PRE-TABLE BLOCK -- #524 armbdt, TABLE 5
=======================================
SEPARATE FILE, not an append.

*** THIS TABLE ADDS NO ROWS AND NO MUTANTS.  IT IS A RE-MEASUREMENT AFTER A
DEFECT WAS FOUND IN THE INSTRUMENT ITSELF. ***

WHAT WENT WRONG.  Table 4 reported BASE 31/32 -- the FIX failing one row,
ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2.  A red BASE row blocks the round, so it was
diagnosed rather than explained away.

I first tried to reason from the byte patterns and COULD NOT MAKE THE
ARITHMETIC CLOSE: under HEAD that row implied the store ended at 0x21004
(base 0x21001), while ARM_STM_COLD_DATA calibrates correctly at base 0x21001.
Two readings of one mechanism that cannot both be true.

SO I INSTRUMENTED INSTEAD, and the dump settled it in one run:

    r0 = 0x21005                                   <- the literal loaded fine
    0x00021000  00000000 44332211 00000000 ...     <- the store LANDED at
                                                      0x21004, exactly as the
                                                      mask intends

THE STORE WAS CORRECT ALL ALONG.  THE READ WAS WRONG.

THE INSTRUMENT DEFECT, measured directly:

    dump from an ALIGNED start
      0x00021000  00000000 44332211
    dump from an UNALIGNED start (what read_mem32 issued)
      0x00021000           44332211 00000000
                  ^^^^^^^^ leading group BLANK-PADDED

read_mem32() searched for the line labelled (addr & ~0xF) -- which matches in
both cases -- then indexed `line[12:48].split()` by (addr & 0xF) // 4.  When
the dump starts unaligned the leading groups are ABSENT and .split() collapses
the padding, so every index shifts and parts[idx] returns a NEIGHBOURING word.

    read_mem32(0x21004) -> parts == ['44332211', '00000000'], idx 1 -> 0

REPAIR: dump from (addr & ~0xF) for 16 bytes, so all four groups are always
present -- which is what the idx arithmetic already assumed.  One line.

SCOPE.  This is a defect in the WITNESS, not in the emulator, and it was
present in the probe as originally built.  It affects every read_mem32 call
at a nonzero 16-byte offset -- i.e. the STM and Thumb PUSH clobber rows --
so ANY previously reported value from those rows is suspect and this table
re-measures all of them.  Grepped: no other probe in the tree shares the
shape.

WHAT MUST BE TRUE FOR #524 TO SHIP
  BASE            CLEAN.  Anything else and the round holds.
  M_MASK1         RED on the P2 rows
  M_MASK7         RED on the BIT2 rows
  M_PBITCOND      RED on THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2
  RESTORED        == HEAD
Predictions only; measured values go to SCORES.txt and citations name it.
